WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:11.700
Yeah, so today we begin the
second part of the class,

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:14.200
specifically up
until last week, we

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:18.620
were focused on the bottom
half of our usual roadmap,

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:22.120
specifically where we focused
on methods for optimal control.

00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:25.320
So if you recall, we
started our discussion

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:27.800
through open loop methods
and namely through the lens

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:29.920
of indirect and
direct methods, where

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:33.400
the goal was to compute an
optimal trajectory from a given

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:35.480
initial condition.

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:39.383
We then-- this is great because
they are fast and reliable,

00:00:39.383 --> 00:00:41.800
but they are specific to the
given initial condition where

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:42.920
we are in.

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:46.560
We then move towards the
closed loop section, namely

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:49.400
through dynamic programming
and Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:53.240
or Hamilton-Jacobi-Isaacs
equations, where there the focus

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:56.740
was on computing an optimal
control policy, that is,

00:00:56.740 --> 00:00:59.760
a function that maps
states to optimal controls.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:02.820
And we saw how this was
definitely more powerful,

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:05.980
at least in principle, compared
to the open loop framework,

00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:10.360
because it gives us a rule of
how to behave independently

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:12.320
on the state in which we are in.

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:15.720
But at the same time, this
came with an important kind

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:18.680
of unimportant
computational expense.

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:21.880
And so, last week,
we focused on MPC

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:24.040
or Model Predictive
Control, which is basically

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:26.920
a framework which tries to
get the best of these two

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:30.200
approaches, namely achieve
the performance of closed loop

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:34.000
methods through the recursive
solution of open loop optimal

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:36.920
control problems.

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:40.520
Underlying all of these
methods was the assumptions

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:42.940
that we knew how the
dynamics behaved.

00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:45.320
So we always knew that we
had a good representation

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:49.280
of our dynamics, which came from
maybe an analytical expression

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:52.640
or a differential equation
that represents our dynamics.

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:54.580
And so in the second
part of this course,

00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:57.000
we relaxed this
assumption, and we

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:58.650
move into the
learning based setting

00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:01.010
where we assume we do
not know in advance

00:02:01.010 --> 00:02:04.570
the dynamics of our models.

00:02:04.570 --> 00:02:06.570
Yes.

00:02:06.570 --> 00:02:09.110
And so, specifically, for
today, we will be, first of all,

00:02:09.110 --> 00:02:11.650
transitioning into the
learning-based setup,

00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:16.490
giving a rough idea of the kind
of topics we'll be discussing.

00:02:16.490 --> 00:02:19.170
And then we will be
approaching specifically

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:21.930
the top left part of
the quadrant, which

00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:28.130
relates with more classical
ways of dealing with learning

00:02:28.130 --> 00:02:31.450
specifically regarding
system identification

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:32.950
and adaptive control.

00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:35.430
And we'll see two examples
of adaptive controls,

00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:38.410
namely MRAC or Model
Reference Adaptive Control

00:02:38.410 --> 00:02:41.430
or MAIC, Model Identification
Adaptive Control.

00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:49.970
And as a general setting,
so remember the framework

00:02:49.970 --> 00:02:51.070
the setting we are in.

00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:55.890
So specifically, we look
at controlling some system

00:02:55.890 --> 00:03:00.930
through the definition of a
controller or a controller.

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:03.110
And in order to
control the system,

00:03:03.110 --> 00:03:06.490
we may be measuring-- we having
some sensors which measure

00:03:06.490 --> 00:03:08.250
the output of our system.

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:11.165
And we might want to compare
the measurements that

00:03:11.165 --> 00:03:13.790
come from our sensor with some
reference which you have in mind

00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:15.930
that is an ideal
behavior of our system

00:03:15.930 --> 00:03:19.490
that we would want
it to execute.

00:03:19.490 --> 00:03:22.230
And this will lead the
construction of our controller.

00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:24.010
And so we have
this feedback loop,

00:03:24.010 --> 00:03:27.123
which happens in closed loop.

00:03:27.123 --> 00:03:29.290
However, the question is,
what happens in the moment

00:03:29.290 --> 00:03:32.130
where we have some uncertainty
or some unmodeled effects

00:03:32.130 --> 00:03:33.770
in how the system behaves?

00:03:33.770 --> 00:03:35.290
And so this is
exactly the setting

00:03:35.290 --> 00:03:37.050
in which we'll be
working on today

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:38.470
and for the rest of this class.

00:03:41.850 --> 00:03:44.530
And so, fundamentally,
so when looking

00:03:44.530 --> 00:03:46.730
at dealing with this
kind of uncertainty that

00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:51.610
could be present in our
system, how can we handle this?

00:03:51.610 --> 00:03:55.780
And so a simple approach
could be that in many cases,

00:03:55.780 --> 00:03:58.360
uncertainties may have
only a small effect.

00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:01.140
So, for example, consider
we're trying to fly a drone

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:04.060
and we might have some
unmodeled wind effects which

00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:06.060
are still pretty small.

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:08.900
And so in this case,
perhaps we don't really

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:11.700
need to do anything too
sophisticated about dealing

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:12.960
with this uncertainty.

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:15.940
But having a feedback
controller may

00:04:15.940 --> 00:04:19.380
be just enough to compensate
for this unmodeled effects.

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:21.220
And so this could
be as simple as we

00:04:21.220 --> 00:04:23.140
have some reference
trajectory for our drone

00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:24.780
that we want to follow.

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:28.220
And we might want to build
a feedback controller that

00:04:28.220 --> 00:04:30.560
tracks this reference
trajectory, for example,

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:32.023
through PID control.

00:04:32.023 --> 00:04:33.940
But again, we're in this
system where we might

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:35.440
have some unmodeled effects.

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:37.820
If these are pretty small,
then maybe feedback control

00:04:37.820 --> 00:04:38.600
is good enough.

00:04:42.620 --> 00:04:44.340
A second approach
is we could try

00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:46.100
to be a little bit
more sophisticated

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:49.540
and going into, for example,
robust control approaches.

00:04:49.540 --> 00:04:54.320
So we discussed last week
or a couple of weeks back,

00:04:54.320 --> 00:05:00.680
if you remember,
min-max formulation,

00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:08.940
where our goal was to maximize
some measure of reward, which

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:14.460
had a form of this type,
maximize with respect

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:15.820
to our controls, u.

00:05:15.820 --> 00:05:20.580
And we were assuming there
was some distribution, gamma

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:23.740
set of distributions,
gamma, which were basically

00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:26.460
playing the role of the
adversary so essentially

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:28.780
modeling the worst case
possible disturbance

00:05:28.780 --> 00:05:30.820
that could affect my system.

00:05:30.820 --> 00:05:33.620
And, in class, we were
looking at this relatively

00:05:33.620 --> 00:05:36.660
sadistic example of the
homicidal chauffeur.

00:05:36.660 --> 00:05:41.380
But this is if we see this from
the perspective of dealing with

00:05:41.380 --> 00:05:45.700
uncertainty, it is as if we're
saying we have a system with

00:05:45.700 --> 00:05:48.740
some uncertainty, which we are
not aware of, things we cannot

00:05:48.740 --> 00:05:49.620
model.

00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:52.020
And so let us be
robust with respect

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:55.590
to this uncertainty by
reasoning with respect

00:05:55.590 --> 00:05:58.350
to the worst case
scenario possible.

00:05:58.350 --> 00:06:00.770
And so, of course, this
is on one hand great.

00:06:00.770 --> 00:06:02.830
But it could come
with behavior that

00:06:02.830 --> 00:06:09.750
is potentially too defensive and
a little bit to conservative.

00:06:09.750 --> 00:06:12.470
But again, this is a
valid second high level

00:06:12.470 --> 00:06:16.270
strategy of approaches.

00:06:16.270 --> 00:06:18.270
The third one, which
is also the one

00:06:18.270 --> 00:06:21.710
we'll be specifically focusing
on today and for the remaining

00:06:21.710 --> 00:06:25.350
weeks, is the one where
we have some uncertainty

00:06:25.350 --> 00:06:30.190
and we take into consideration
the fact that we can collect

00:06:30.190 --> 00:06:36.230
examples of behavior and state
transitions in our environment.

00:06:36.230 --> 00:06:41.430
So again, consider the case
where we have our drone flying

00:06:41.430 --> 00:06:42.990
to accomplish some task.

00:06:42.990 --> 00:06:45.990
We might be storing
logs of previous flights

00:06:45.990 --> 00:06:48.990
in order to observe
these state transitions

00:06:48.990 --> 00:06:52.110
and use those to learn from
data and approximate model

00:06:52.110 --> 00:06:54.350
of our dynamics.

00:06:54.350 --> 00:06:58.050
And so this is exactly the
setting we'll be working with.

00:06:58.050 --> 00:07:01.350
But I think this is a
nice frame mental model

00:07:01.350 --> 00:07:03.110
to have in the
sense that whenever

00:07:03.110 --> 00:07:05.270
we have some
uncertainty, we might

00:07:05.270 --> 00:07:07.430
be good enough
with some feedback

00:07:07.430 --> 00:07:09.790
that in order to correct
for our model effects,

00:07:09.790 --> 00:07:13.190
we might want to be
extremely conservative

00:07:13.190 --> 00:07:15.650
and reason about worst
case scenario possible.

00:07:15.650 --> 00:07:17.870
Or we could use data
driven approaches

00:07:17.870 --> 00:07:21.310
that leverage pre-collected
state transitions observations

00:07:21.310 --> 00:07:24.210
in order to learn an approximate
model of our dynamics.

00:07:26.910 --> 00:07:30.430
Any questions on these high
level and macro categories

00:07:30.430 --> 00:07:31.530
of methods?

00:07:39.790 --> 00:07:43.890
So in the moment we zoom in our
third of these macro categories,

00:07:43.890 --> 00:07:47.350
that is data-driven methods,
the question that comes to mind

00:07:47.350 --> 00:07:50.160
is, OK, we say we want
to learn something,

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:52.040
but what can we learn?

00:07:52.040 --> 00:07:55.320
And at a high level, I would
say that different methods

00:07:55.320 --> 00:07:57.480
for learning based
control typically

00:07:57.480 --> 00:08:02.440
divide themselves across two
main families of approaches.

00:08:02.440 --> 00:08:04.440
In the first one, we
want to use measurements

00:08:04.440 --> 00:08:07.160
or collected episodes
or transitions

00:08:07.160 --> 00:08:11.400
in order to directly
improve the controller.

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:14.080
On the other hand, we might
want to use these measurements

00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:17.920
to learn an approximate
model of our dynamics

00:08:17.920 --> 00:08:20.920
and then use this model
to approve the controller.

00:08:20.920 --> 00:08:25.040
So you see that in the
second of these two families,

00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:26.880
we have this intermediate
objective, which

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:31.240
is system identification,
in a way, which

00:08:31.240 --> 00:08:34.740
is then a proxy to improve
our overall controller.

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:39.919
And so the types
of methods that we

00:08:39.919 --> 00:08:43.240
will see today and for the
rest of this class loosely

00:08:43.240 --> 00:08:45.840
fall within these two buckets.

00:08:45.840 --> 00:08:49.720
And so, today, we'll be focusing
on at direct adaptive control

00:08:49.720 --> 00:08:53.782
and indirect adaptive control as
well as system identification.

00:08:53.782 --> 00:08:55.240
But in the coming
weeks, we'll also

00:08:55.240 --> 00:08:58.160
focus on imitation learning as
well as model free and model

00:08:58.160 --> 00:08:59.940
based reinforcement learning.

00:09:12.680 --> 00:09:15.920
As a second question that
comes to mind would be then,

00:09:15.920 --> 00:09:18.380
how does learning
actually happen?

00:09:18.380 --> 00:09:20.600
And specifically,
there are three

00:09:20.600 --> 00:09:22.460
I would say prototypical cases.

00:09:22.460 --> 00:09:25.740
The first one is
the 0 episodes case,

00:09:25.740 --> 00:09:28.360
which is also known as the
kind of system identification

00:09:28.360 --> 00:09:29.060
approach.

00:09:29.060 --> 00:09:32.960
And the assumption here is
that we have, in some way,

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:36.700
pre-collected a data set of
state transitions or rollouts,

00:09:36.700 --> 00:09:40.000
if you wish, of some policy
controlling our system.

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:43.320
And we might want to use
this offline data set

00:09:43.320 --> 00:09:44.980
to improve our controller.

00:09:44.980 --> 00:09:47.470
This could be directly
improving the controller

00:09:47.470 --> 00:09:50.650
or it could be learning a model
to then improve the controller.

00:09:50.650 --> 00:09:52.610
But what I want to
stress the fact here

00:09:52.610 --> 00:09:55.370
is that this zero
episode case essentially

00:09:55.370 --> 00:09:58.330
entails having some mechanism
of collecting a data

00:09:58.330 --> 00:10:00.750
set of completely offline.

00:10:00.750 --> 00:10:03.090
And then we use this
data set to estimate

00:10:03.090 --> 00:10:06.570
some parameters or some models
in order to improve controller

00:10:06.570 --> 00:10:09.170
performance.

00:10:09.170 --> 00:10:12.130
This is to be distinguished
from the one episode case,

00:10:12.130 --> 00:10:15.410
where we want to
have this adaptation

00:10:15.410 --> 00:10:19.330
and learning happening directly
online as a kind of while we

00:10:19.330 --> 00:10:21.370
control the system.

00:10:21.370 --> 00:10:24.790
And I think, kind of again,
taking our drone example,

00:10:24.790 --> 00:10:28.370
say that we have
some quadrotor that

00:10:28.370 --> 00:10:32.390
is carrying some payload
of an unknown mass,

00:10:32.390 --> 00:10:36.272
m, the one episode or
online adaptation approach

00:10:36.272 --> 00:10:37.730
would essentially
mean that we have

00:10:37.730 --> 00:10:43.313
no mechanism of estimating this
mass, m, from previous flights.

00:10:43.313 --> 00:10:44.730
But this is something
that we have

00:10:44.730 --> 00:10:49.450
to estimate and adapt to online
while we control the drone.

00:10:49.450 --> 00:10:52.370
And so the goal here would be
to while we are controlling

00:10:52.370 --> 00:10:56.390
the drone, estimate some unknown
parameters such as, for example,

00:10:56.390 --> 00:10:58.470
the unknown mass of our payload.

00:11:02.530 --> 00:11:05.170
And then a third
category of approaches

00:11:05.170 --> 00:11:09.330
is one of the multiple
episodes setting.

00:11:09.330 --> 00:11:11.050
And this is the
classical framework

00:11:11.050 --> 00:11:12.890
for reinforcement learning.

00:11:12.890 --> 00:11:16.010
The idea here is that we have
some environment with which we

00:11:16.010 --> 00:11:20.650
can interact with,
say, for example,

00:11:20.650 --> 00:11:25.290
we want to learn a policy
that plays chess optimally.

00:11:25.290 --> 00:11:27.730
This is we have an
environment which is

00:11:27.730 --> 00:11:29.350
the game environment of chess.

00:11:29.350 --> 00:11:32.610
And these environments
can be reset indefinitely.

00:11:32.610 --> 00:11:36.650
And here learning happens
in between these episodes.

00:11:36.650 --> 00:11:38.250
So we could have
a situation where

00:11:38.250 --> 00:11:41.850
we spawn our environment at the
beginning of the chess game.

00:11:41.850 --> 00:11:44.060
We interact with the
environment in order

00:11:44.060 --> 00:11:45.780
to complete one episode.

00:11:45.780 --> 00:11:48.640
And then this gets
reset at the beginning.

00:11:48.640 --> 00:11:52.500
And we use these repetitions
of different strategies

00:11:52.500 --> 00:11:55.840
in order to update our
parameters or policies,

00:11:55.840 --> 00:11:59.420
depending on what
we're trying to do.

00:11:59.420 --> 00:12:02.940
But I hope that is clear
that the point of this slide

00:12:02.940 --> 00:12:06.740
is that once we choose that
we want to learn and approach

00:12:06.740 --> 00:12:11.260
control through learning,
then different algorithms will

00:12:11.260 --> 00:12:13.540
be better suited for
different settings

00:12:13.540 --> 00:12:16.900
depending on if we assume
we have collected some data

00:12:16.900 --> 00:12:20.680
set, some offline data set
beforehand or on the other hand,

00:12:20.680 --> 00:12:22.420
whether we want to
control some system

00:12:22.420 --> 00:12:24.940
and adapt to unmodeled
effects directly

00:12:24.940 --> 00:12:27.820
while we are deploying
and operating our system.

00:12:27.820 --> 00:12:30.420
Or in the last case,
one of the multiple

00:12:30.420 --> 00:12:32.420
episodes where we
have an environment

00:12:32.420 --> 00:12:35.440
with which we have the luxury
of being able to interact with.

00:12:39.580 --> 00:12:44.180
Of course, the boundaries
here are not so strict.

00:12:44.180 --> 00:12:46.400
Methods fall between
different categories.

00:12:46.400 --> 00:12:49.380
So, for example, I would say
online reinforcement learning

00:12:49.380 --> 00:12:51.500
typically assumes we have
this environment we can

00:12:51.500 --> 00:12:54.520
reset and iteratively adapt to.

00:12:54.520 --> 00:12:56.195
But there are methods
that still belong

00:12:56.195 --> 00:12:57.820
to the realm of
reinforcement learning,

00:12:57.820 --> 00:12:59.500
say offline
reinforcement learning,

00:12:59.500 --> 00:13:01.840
which actually fall in
the zero episode case.

00:13:01.840 --> 00:13:03.800
So don't take this
too literally.

00:13:03.800 --> 00:13:06.060
But I think, typically,
you have these three

00:13:06.060 --> 00:13:10.160
very specific modalities of
experience which come into play.

00:13:16.020 --> 00:13:17.080
Any questions here?

00:13:21.060 --> 00:13:22.380
Yes.

00:13:22.380 --> 00:13:25.660
Can you do this between one
episode and multiple episodes?

00:13:25.660 --> 00:13:28.780
Multiple episodes is capable of
interacting with the environment

00:13:28.780 --> 00:13:30.760
and learning through
those interactions.

00:13:30.760 --> 00:13:34.400
So I would say that also
in the one episode case,

00:13:34.400 --> 00:13:36.380
you interact with
the environment.

00:13:36.380 --> 00:13:38.020
In the multiple
episodes case, I would

00:13:38.020 --> 00:13:42.260
say the difference is that you
have this episodic nature where

00:13:42.260 --> 00:13:44.860
you can learn across
repeated episodes.

00:13:44.860 --> 00:13:47.220
So again, I think a good
example here is chess.

00:13:47.220 --> 00:13:50.260
So if I'm trying to learn
a policy to play chess,

00:13:50.260 --> 00:13:52.620
I could play an
infinite amount of games

00:13:52.620 --> 00:13:55.980
and I improve my policy
across these games.

00:13:55.980 --> 00:13:58.400
Here, if we take the
same chess analogy,

00:13:58.400 --> 00:14:00.660
it would be that I'm trying
to learn how to play chess

00:14:00.660 --> 00:14:01.840
during a single game.

00:14:04.860 --> 00:14:06.768
Sometimes with
multiple episodes,

00:14:06.768 --> 00:14:09.060
that's the only way you can
get to other states, right?

00:14:09.060 --> 00:14:10.268
Sorry, could you repeat that?

00:14:10.268 --> 00:14:12.280
If you have a policy,
if you run it once,

00:14:12.280 --> 00:14:13.900
you can only get
to the states you

00:14:13.900 --> 00:14:17.020
go to in that one versus getting
to different states [INAUDIBLE]?

00:14:17.020 --> 00:14:18.280
In the one episode case?

00:14:20.860 --> 00:14:23.300
No, I'm asking like that's how
you get to the other states

00:14:23.300 --> 00:14:26.300
that you wouldn't get if
you go one time through.

00:14:26.300 --> 00:14:28.442
Yeah, so in the
multiple episodes case,

00:14:28.442 --> 00:14:30.400
if you act differently
in the same environment,

00:14:30.400 --> 00:14:32.940
you would be able to explore
different parts of your state

00:14:32.940 --> 00:14:37.260
space if this is what
you're referring to.

00:14:37.260 --> 00:14:39.180
Yes.

00:14:39.180 --> 00:14:41.190
Episodes, if you
deploy a vehicle

00:14:41.190 --> 00:14:43.830
and then take different
time horizons which

00:14:43.830 --> 00:14:45.710
do different
actions, could those

00:14:45.710 --> 00:14:49.590
be considered different episodes
or is that still one episode?

00:14:49.590 --> 00:14:55.270
So are you referring to an
RL-like approach where I want--

00:14:55.270 --> 00:14:59.730
[INAUDIBLE] the
dynamics of the vehicle.

00:14:59.730 --> 00:15:04.590
For instance, would you
cut up one piloted thing

00:15:04.590 --> 00:15:07.270
of some operation into
multiple time histories

00:15:07.270 --> 00:15:08.970
and then use that as
multiple episodes?

00:15:08.970 --> 00:15:10.550
Or is that still one episode?

00:15:10.550 --> 00:15:14.050
I see, I would probably classify
this in the zero episode case,

00:15:14.050 --> 00:15:17.390
meaning that you have a
data set of trajectories

00:15:17.390 --> 00:15:19.590
and you're basically
chunking this up in order

00:15:19.590 --> 00:15:24.110
to build a next state prediction
or a forward dynamics problem,

00:15:24.110 --> 00:15:25.270
if that makes sense.

00:15:25.270 --> 00:15:29.150
Because you're not really
being necessarily an agent

00:15:29.150 --> 00:15:30.470
in collecting that data.

00:15:30.470 --> 00:15:34.010
So here in the multiple episode
case, typically your policy,

00:15:34.010 --> 00:15:35.750
your control law
is actually the one

00:15:35.750 --> 00:15:38.830
that dictates the
experience that you have.

00:15:38.830 --> 00:15:40.510
In the zero episode
case, you probably

00:15:40.510 --> 00:15:44.150
don't want a neural
net from scratch

00:15:44.150 --> 00:15:47.510
driving a vehicle because
this will crash, for sure.

00:15:47.510 --> 00:15:49.910
But in what happens
in chess is that you

00:15:49.910 --> 00:15:52.090
have very bad policies
at the beginning,

00:15:52.090 --> 00:15:53.890
and then you improve over time.

00:16:02.710 --> 00:16:05.650
And so given this
broader framework,

00:16:05.650 --> 00:16:09.790
let's approach learning based
control methods, specifically

00:16:09.790 --> 00:16:13.190
through the lens of classical
approaches to learning starting

00:16:13.190 --> 00:16:15.450
from system identification.

00:16:18.350 --> 00:16:22.110
And the problem and the idea
of system identification

00:16:22.110 --> 00:16:26.330
is that for many problems, we
don't have to learn online.

00:16:26.330 --> 00:16:29.470
But we could actually
have a pipeline

00:16:29.470 --> 00:16:34.390
where we collect data set
of previous experiments

00:16:34.390 --> 00:16:37.120
or behaviors in a given
environment or system

00:16:37.120 --> 00:16:38.600
that we care to model.

00:16:38.600 --> 00:16:41.560
And we want to use this data
set to build a data driven

00:16:41.560 --> 00:16:43.180
model of the dynamics.

00:16:46.200 --> 00:16:47.840
So in this way,
what we can then do

00:16:47.840 --> 00:16:52.720
is that we can then use
this data-driven model

00:16:52.720 --> 00:16:54.920
to essentially
replace our knowledge

00:16:54.920 --> 00:16:57.565
of the dynamics for control.

00:16:57.565 --> 00:16:59.940
So the idea is that the overall
problem we want to solve,

00:16:59.940 --> 00:17:02.380
we have a missing
dynamical model.

00:17:02.380 --> 00:17:06.480
We want to learn to approximate
this from some data gathered

00:17:06.480 --> 00:17:11.000
beforehand, and then use this
approximate model as essentially

00:17:11.000 --> 00:17:15.760
proxy for then using any control
tool that we wish to use.

00:17:15.760 --> 00:17:20.560
And the classical, at least
traditionally method or tool

00:17:20.560 --> 00:17:24.359
for system identification
is linear regression.

00:17:24.359 --> 00:17:28.853
And so I'm sure this will be
repetition for most of you.

00:17:28.853 --> 00:17:30.520
But let's just set
the notation straight

00:17:30.520 --> 00:17:35.520
on learning through
least square minimization

00:17:35.520 --> 00:17:40.000
and linear regression,
in particular.

00:17:40.000 --> 00:17:43.360
So specifically, we are
describing a setting

00:17:43.360 --> 00:17:50.560
where we assume that we have
some system model described

00:17:50.560 --> 00:17:56.120
as y equals theta transpose
z plus some noise epsilon,

00:17:56.120 --> 00:17:58.660
where y is the output.

00:18:02.600 --> 00:18:07.480
We have some inputs, z.

00:18:07.480 --> 00:18:10.380
Theta are some parameters
which we wish to learn.

00:18:13.680 --> 00:18:15.920
And then we assume
that the process

00:18:15.920 --> 00:18:18.080
that we wish to
approximate is described

00:18:18.080 --> 00:18:21.020
by some inherent noise, epsilon.

00:18:24.000 --> 00:18:26.440
And the way we want to
approximate the system is

00:18:26.440 --> 00:18:32.000
through examples which we
collect in a data set, d, which

00:18:32.000 --> 00:18:39.290
are nothing else than tuples of
input/output pairs, Zy with ita

00:18:39.290 --> 00:18:42.690
goes from 1 to N data points.

00:18:42.690 --> 00:18:45.810
And we want to use this data
set that we collected offline

00:18:45.810 --> 00:18:49.850
of examples of
input/output realizations

00:18:49.850 --> 00:18:55.970
to learn to estimate an
approximate model of our system.

00:18:55.970 --> 00:19:00.510
Now, typically, the way we use
these examples is through--

00:19:00.510 --> 00:19:02.490
and the way we learn
the parameters, theta,

00:19:02.490 --> 00:19:05.090
to approximate our model
is through the minimization

00:19:05.090 --> 00:19:09.970
of some sort of performance
criterion or loss,

00:19:09.970 --> 00:19:14.010
which could be the
least squares loss.

00:19:14.010 --> 00:19:15.970
So something that
essentially encodes

00:19:15.970 --> 00:19:19.410
the difference between
the true realizations

00:19:19.410 --> 00:19:22.490
and our predictions.

00:19:22.490 --> 00:19:26.050
Something of this form.

00:19:26.050 --> 00:19:31.770
In the results, we are
referring to capital Z

00:19:31.770 --> 00:19:44.970
as the matrix of the
stacked input vectors which

00:19:44.970 --> 00:19:48.130
allows us to rewrite
it in compact form

00:19:48.130 --> 00:19:55.950
as the minimization of the
squared Euclidean norm.

00:20:02.570 --> 00:20:07.290
And from all our
discussions on optimization

00:20:07.290 --> 00:20:08.890
and numerical
optimization, we know

00:20:08.890 --> 00:20:14.530
that this is a convex function,
which we wish to minimize.

00:20:14.530 --> 00:20:17.560
And so we know
that in order to--

00:20:17.560 --> 00:20:26.610
let's go here-- to find the
minimum of that function

00:20:26.610 --> 00:20:30.570
we need to set the gradient
of the objective with respect

00:20:30.570 --> 00:20:31.960
to theta equal to 0.

00:20:41.440 --> 00:20:45.460
So this is our necessary
sufficient condition

00:20:45.460 --> 00:20:51.540
for optimality,
which essentially

00:20:51.540 --> 00:20:54.980
means that we want to take
the gradient of y transpose

00:20:54.980 --> 00:21:03.900
y minus 2 theta transpose z
transpose y plus theta transpose

00:21:03.900 --> 00:21:07.900
Z transpose Z theta.

00:21:07.900 --> 00:21:09.520
And we want to set this to 0.

00:21:14.660 --> 00:21:19.860
And by taking the gradient, what
we get is negative 2Z transpose

00:21:19.860 --> 00:21:27.500
y plus 2Z transpose Z
theta to be equal to 0,

00:21:27.500 --> 00:21:31.940
which essentially leads to
the solution that we have

00:21:31.940 --> 00:21:38.620
on the slide, which is
basically that the value

00:21:38.620 --> 00:21:44.500
of the parameters, theta, that
minimize our least squares error

00:21:44.500 --> 00:21:52.720
is Z transpose the inverse of Z
transpose Z times Z transpose y.

00:22:01.180 --> 00:22:07.440
So nothing to technically deep
or to insightful in this slide,

00:22:07.440 --> 00:22:09.800
but just to make sure we
have the notation in place.

00:22:14.700 --> 00:22:19.580
So we're saying that we want
to use these tools for system

00:22:19.580 --> 00:22:20.920
identification.

00:22:20.920 --> 00:22:24.620
So let us map this
framework into the problem

00:22:24.620 --> 00:22:26.140
that we actually
care about, which

00:22:26.140 --> 00:22:29.890
is using a linear model to
approximate our system dynamics.

00:22:36.150 --> 00:22:38.030
And so, specifically,
what we're saying

00:22:38.030 --> 00:22:43.070
is that we are assuming that
our system evolves according

00:22:43.070 --> 00:22:49.110
to the following
linear system, xt

00:22:49.110 --> 00:23:02.790
plus 1 equals axt plus but plus
some noise, epsilon t, which

00:23:02.790 --> 00:23:10.870
we can rewrite essentially
more compactly as the dot

00:23:10.870 --> 00:23:17.270
product between xtut and vector
ab plus the noise term, epsilon

00:23:17.270 --> 00:23:17.770
t.

00:23:20.710 --> 00:23:22.990
And once we write
it this way, I think

00:23:22.990 --> 00:23:26.150
it's pretty clear to
see that this is what

00:23:26.150 --> 00:23:28.950
we were referring to as our y.

00:23:28.950 --> 00:23:31.310
These are our
inputs, Z. And these

00:23:31.310 --> 00:23:34.870
will be our parameters, theta,
according to the notation

00:23:34.870 --> 00:23:35.970
from the previous slide.

00:23:42.750 --> 00:23:45.910
So, in practice,
what this means is

00:23:45.910 --> 00:23:49.470
that we assume we
have access to a data

00:23:49.470 --> 00:23:55.630
set of previous transitions
or rollouts of our system.

00:23:55.630 --> 00:23:57.830
So, if you wish,
we are assuming we

00:23:57.830 --> 00:24:00.350
have collected some
trajectories that

00:24:00.350 --> 00:24:03.950
represent possible
evolutions of our systems.

00:24:03.950 --> 00:24:06.190
These trajectories
are nothing else

00:24:06.190 --> 00:24:18.230
than collections of states
and controls over time.

00:24:18.230 --> 00:24:20.950
And we can then use
these trajectories

00:24:20.950 --> 00:24:24.150
that we collected in order
to basically construct

00:24:24.150 --> 00:24:30.760
a data set, which
again, is tuples

00:24:30.760 --> 00:24:37.240
of xtut, which are our
inputs, and xt plus 1,

00:24:37.240 --> 00:24:39.060
which will be our output.

00:24:56.280 --> 00:24:58.520
So this is what we
mean when we say

00:24:58.520 --> 00:25:03.100
we want to learn an approximate
model of our dynamics from data.

00:25:03.100 --> 00:25:05.800
That is, we will
have some mechanism

00:25:05.800 --> 00:25:09.940
to collect examples of
evolutions of the system.

00:25:09.940 --> 00:25:13.000
And we repurposed these
examples, essentially,

00:25:13.000 --> 00:25:16.880
as a regression
problem, which maps

00:25:16.880 --> 00:25:20.260
from current state and current
control to the next state.

00:25:25.720 --> 00:25:29.080
Any questions on this?

00:25:29.080 --> 00:25:33.500
I'm sure this is probably
just a background for you,

00:25:33.500 --> 00:25:37.520
but just to make sure we
are all on the same page.

00:25:37.520 --> 00:25:40.940
So some comments,
so in practice,

00:25:40.940 --> 00:25:45.960
we have shown how the
solution to our least

00:25:45.960 --> 00:25:51.040
squares optimization entails
this inverse over Z transpose Z.

00:25:51.040 --> 00:25:55.860
Depending on how large our data
set is, Z could be very large.

00:25:55.860 --> 00:25:58.800
And so doing this inverse can
also be computationally quite

00:25:58.800 --> 00:25:59.680
expensive.

00:25:59.680 --> 00:26:04.320
And so there exists a number of
recursive formulations or forms

00:26:04.320 --> 00:26:10.160
for this optimal solution,
which essentially

00:26:10.160 --> 00:26:15.200
enable us to avoid
this recursive inverse

00:26:15.200 --> 00:26:18.240
of a potentially very large
matrix that could be extremely

00:26:18.240 --> 00:26:19.580
expensive in practice.

00:26:19.580 --> 00:26:22.770
So this is very useful if we
have a stream of data coming in,

00:26:22.770 --> 00:26:27.202
and we don't want to resolve
this recompute this every time.

00:26:27.202 --> 00:26:29.410
And, of course, here we're
dealing with discrete time

00:26:29.410 --> 00:26:31.290
formulation, but
there exist equivalent

00:26:31.290 --> 00:26:32.870
also in continuous time.

00:26:38.770 --> 00:26:42.890
So here just a couple
of theoretical results

00:26:42.890 --> 00:26:44.350
you might be familiar with.

00:26:44.350 --> 00:26:48.090
So the first one is that
this estimator is actually

00:26:48.090 --> 00:26:49.730
turns out that it
is actually the best

00:26:49.730 --> 00:26:54.970
linear unbiased estimator
for any noise distribution

00:26:54.970 --> 00:26:57.490
that obeys some common
assumptions specifically

00:26:57.490 --> 00:27:03.090
that the noise terms, epsilon
t, are 0 mean uncorrelated,

00:27:03.090 --> 00:27:05.190
and all have the
same finite variance.

00:27:05.190 --> 00:27:07.930
So you might be familiar with
this from your statistics

00:27:07.930 --> 00:27:09.170
classes.

00:27:09.170 --> 00:27:11.490
And if the noise
distribution is Gaussian,

00:27:11.490 --> 00:27:14.850
specifically, then this
estimator that we just derived

00:27:14.850 --> 00:27:17.390
is also known as the maximum
likelihood estimator.

00:27:23.050 --> 00:27:26.730
OK, so a question that we
might want to ask ourselves

00:27:26.730 --> 00:27:30.050
the moment we decide that this
is the mechanism for which we

00:27:30.050 --> 00:27:32.490
are trying to estimate
the unknown parameters

00:27:32.490 --> 00:27:34.730
is, what are
sufficient conditions

00:27:34.730 --> 00:27:37.390
for which our
estimator, theta hat,

00:27:37.390 --> 00:27:41.570
converges to the ground truth to
the known and true parameters,

00:27:41.570 --> 00:27:43.730
theta?

00:27:43.730 --> 00:27:46.290
So in other words,
what we're saying

00:27:46.290 --> 00:27:50.910
is that say we have
an x-axis here,

00:27:50.910 --> 00:27:55.410
which represents the data, the
amount of data that we collect.

00:27:55.410 --> 00:27:57.730
And we have the y-axis
here that represents

00:27:57.730 --> 00:28:02.130
our estimator, theta hat.

00:28:02.130 --> 00:28:05.570
And consider a specific value
for the ground truth value

00:28:05.570 --> 00:28:08.650
of our parameters, theta star.

00:28:08.650 --> 00:28:10.850
What we wish to
know is we could see

00:28:10.850 --> 00:28:15.130
this process of estimating
theta hat is itself

00:28:15.130 --> 00:28:17.410
as a random variable.

00:28:17.410 --> 00:28:19.910
And what we might want
to know is, first of all,

00:28:19.910 --> 00:28:22.710
if given more data,
does this process,

00:28:22.710 --> 00:28:27.410
is it centered on the
ground truth value of theta?

00:28:27.410 --> 00:28:29.850
And also how does
this estimation

00:28:29.850 --> 00:28:31.790
vary the more data we collect?

00:28:35.770 --> 00:28:38.970
And so the reason why I'm
saying that we could see this

00:28:38.970 --> 00:28:40.930
as a realization
of random variable

00:28:40.930 --> 00:28:43.370
is because from the
definition, which we just

00:28:43.370 --> 00:28:48.010
derived of our solution, we
see how our estimator theta

00:28:48.010 --> 00:28:51.650
hat depends on the
true output, y,

00:28:51.650 --> 00:28:54.590
which is a process that
depends on noise epsilon.

00:28:54.590 --> 00:28:59.250
So we defined before
Ry as theta transpose Z

00:28:59.250 --> 00:29:01.810
plus some noise epsilon.

00:29:01.810 --> 00:29:05.190
And so by just doing the
appropriate substitutions--

00:29:08.890 --> 00:29:12.770
so here we're just replacing
the definition of y.

00:29:12.770 --> 00:29:16.730
And by just doing reassembling
the terms, what we get

00:29:16.730 --> 00:29:21.900
is that we can rewrite our
estimator, theta hat, as theta

00:29:21.900 --> 00:29:25.940
plus the inverse of Z
transpose Z Z transpose

00:29:25.940 --> 00:29:27.780
times the noise epsilon.

00:29:27.780 --> 00:29:30.580
So epsilon is, indeed, some--

00:29:30.580 --> 00:29:35.620
this is what makes our estimator
itself a random variable.

00:29:35.620 --> 00:29:37.840
And so if this is
a random variable,

00:29:37.840 --> 00:29:41.060
we might want to study the kind
of questions we are asking over

00:29:41.060 --> 00:29:44.540
here on the behavior
of our estimator

00:29:44.540 --> 00:29:46.820
through the lens of
looking at the moments

00:29:46.820 --> 00:29:48.800
of this random variable.

00:29:48.800 --> 00:29:54.240
So, specifically, the mean and
covariance of our estimator.

00:29:57.740 --> 00:29:59.800
Is it clear what we
are trying to do?

00:30:05.586 --> 00:30:10.480
So looking at the mean
first, first of all,

00:30:10.480 --> 00:30:14.180
we simply replace
our new definition

00:30:14.180 --> 00:30:17.320
of the estimator of theta
hat inside the expectation.

00:30:21.620 --> 00:30:27.000
Since the only random variable
here is the noise term, epsilon,

00:30:27.000 --> 00:30:32.620
we can basically take everything
out of the expectation operator.

00:30:32.620 --> 00:30:36.620
And we see how we can use
the assumption that our noise

00:30:36.620 --> 00:30:41.800
terms are zero mean to basically
cancel out this right term.

00:30:41.800 --> 00:30:45.420
And so what we get is that the
expectation of our estimator

00:30:45.420 --> 00:30:48.500
is indeed the true value of
the parameters theta, which

00:30:48.500 --> 00:30:51.340
is a nice result.
So what we know here

00:30:51.340 --> 00:30:55.140
is that in
expectation, if we keep

00:30:55.140 --> 00:30:58.120
using our estimator of the
true parameters, theta,

00:30:58.120 --> 00:31:01.420
we will converge to
the true value, theta.

00:31:01.420 --> 00:31:03.260
So in this case,
we basically proven

00:31:03.260 --> 00:31:06.000
that the more data we
collect in expectation,

00:31:06.000 --> 00:31:08.980
the more we will actually be
converging to the ground truth

00:31:08.980 --> 00:31:10.400
value of theta.

00:31:15.670 --> 00:31:18.210
So similarly, we can
look at the covariance,

00:31:18.210 --> 00:31:22.870
so how our estimation oscillates
around the true value of theta.

00:31:22.870 --> 00:31:26.830
And we can do, again,
some algebraic steps.

00:31:26.830 --> 00:31:30.110
So first of all, we again,
just rewrite the definition

00:31:30.110 --> 00:31:32.310
of theta hat.

00:31:32.310 --> 00:31:35.310
And then we use
this useful identity

00:31:35.310 --> 00:31:40.950
that the covariance of ax is
equal to a covariance of XA

00:31:40.950 --> 00:31:42.550
transpose.

00:31:42.550 --> 00:31:48.910
And here, specifically, we
define A as Z transpose Z.

00:31:48.910 --> 00:31:51.790
So the inverse of Z transpose
Z is Z transpose and X

00:31:51.790 --> 00:31:53.730
as our noise epsilon.

00:32:01.590 --> 00:32:05.910
And so what we get is
this second row over here,

00:32:05.910 --> 00:32:08.670
and we use, again,
one of the assumptions

00:32:08.670 --> 00:32:13.630
that we were seeing before on
how the noise distribution is

00:32:13.630 --> 00:32:14.330
defined.

00:32:14.330 --> 00:32:16.210
And specifically,
we were assuming,

00:32:16.210 --> 00:32:22.830
if you recall from
the previous slide,

00:32:22.830 --> 00:32:27.430
that all the noise terms,
epsilon T, are uncorrelated

00:32:27.430 --> 00:32:30.230
and all have the
same finite variance.

00:32:30.230 --> 00:32:33.110
So this essentially means
that our covariance matrix

00:32:33.110 --> 00:32:35.470
for the epsilon
values is something

00:32:35.470 --> 00:32:40.310
that only has zero
terms everywhere

00:32:40.310 --> 00:32:46.690
except from the diagonal where
we have the same variance where

00:32:46.690 --> 00:32:54.150
here we're assuming the
variance is sigma squared, which

00:32:54.150 --> 00:32:57.650
essentially means that we can
do this substitution over there.

00:33:00.350 --> 00:33:02.270
So again, doing some
algebraic steps,

00:33:02.270 --> 00:33:04.230
I think the bottom
line is that we

00:33:04.230 --> 00:33:06.830
get an expression of
our covariance, which

00:33:06.830 --> 00:33:10.590
is the variance sigma
squared multiplied

00:33:10.590 --> 00:33:12.760
by the inverse of
a summation terms

00:33:12.760 --> 00:33:16.360
that goes from 1 to N of
basically different combinations

00:33:16.360 --> 00:33:19.680
of our states and controls.

00:33:19.680 --> 00:33:24.440
Now, from the perspective
of our estimation process,

00:33:24.440 --> 00:33:27.040
we would like the
covariance to basically go

00:33:27.040 --> 00:33:29.320
to 0 the more data we collect.

00:33:29.320 --> 00:33:31.800
So we want our estimation
to be more and more centered

00:33:31.800 --> 00:33:35.920
around our expectation.

00:33:35.920 --> 00:33:37.800
And so this means
that in practice,

00:33:37.800 --> 00:33:40.480
since this is an
inverse, and so it

00:33:40.480 --> 00:33:42.520
goes at the denominator,
what we want

00:33:42.520 --> 00:33:45.240
is that this summation
from 1 to N--

00:33:45.240 --> 00:33:47.880
the more N grows this
has to go to infinity

00:33:47.880 --> 00:33:50.520
so that the overall
thing goes to 0.

00:33:50.520 --> 00:33:52.600
And so what we're saying
is that, first of all,

00:33:52.600 --> 00:33:58.040
this is a neat result again
in the sense that as long as--

00:33:58.040 --> 00:34:01.600
if our system operates
forever, and it operates

00:34:01.600 --> 00:34:05.840
in a non-trivial way, meaning
that the X is the nu that we

00:34:05.840 --> 00:34:10.320
observe are non-trivial, for
example, just statically stop

00:34:10.320 --> 00:34:13.860
to 0, then that summation
will go to infinity,

00:34:13.860 --> 00:34:15.659
and so our covariance
will go to 0.

00:34:19.920 --> 00:34:27.360
So here I meant what I
refer to as non-trivially,

00:34:27.360 --> 00:34:29.679
non-trivially operating
the system what

00:34:29.679 --> 00:34:33.360
I meant is that I refer to this
characteristic that is known

00:34:33.360 --> 00:34:36.040
as persistent excitation,
which essentially encodes

00:34:36.040 --> 00:34:38.520
this idea that we have
to continuously probe

00:34:38.520 --> 00:34:41.179
the system in meaningful ways.

00:34:41.179 --> 00:34:44.639
And if we do so, then
to the limit of N that

00:34:44.639 --> 00:34:46.400
goes to infinity,
also our estimator

00:34:46.400 --> 00:34:50.380
will be exactly around
the true value of theta.

00:34:53.600 --> 00:34:56.600
Any questions here?

00:34:56.600 --> 00:34:58.680
Yes.

00:34:58.680 --> 00:35:00.880
I was wondering, have
you seen intuition

00:35:00.880 --> 00:35:04.600
on why this is invertible
enough [INAUDIBLE]?

00:35:04.600 --> 00:35:05.620
Sorry.

00:35:05.620 --> 00:35:09.120
Is there any [INAUDIBLE] way
this is invertible enough?

00:35:09.120 --> 00:35:12.510
What is the score condition?

00:35:12.510 --> 00:35:15.230
So there is here we're kind
of simplifying the treatment.

00:35:15.230 --> 00:35:17.170
There is some--

00:35:17.170 --> 00:35:20.890
I can definitely refer you
to the complete derivations

00:35:20.890 --> 00:35:24.843
that basically will tell
us when this is invertible.

00:35:24.843 --> 00:35:26.510
But this is just as
a frame of reference

00:35:26.510 --> 00:35:30.970
considering that our goal
here is system dynamics

00:35:30.970 --> 00:35:33.410
and estimating these
true parameters, theta.

00:35:33.410 --> 00:35:38.150
I was just fast tracking
to the overall result,

00:35:38.150 --> 00:35:40.650
saying that if we use
linear models to estimate

00:35:40.650 --> 00:35:44.590
our dynamical systems and we
use this estimator for theta

00:35:44.590 --> 00:35:46.290
then we get something
that is unbiased.

00:35:46.290 --> 00:35:49.690
And that, under some
regularity conditions,

00:35:49.690 --> 00:35:53.690
basically also converges with 0
covariance, if that makes sense.

00:35:53.690 --> 00:35:55.450
So there are some
regularity conditions

00:35:55.450 --> 00:35:58.850
that I'm happy to refer you to.

00:35:58.850 --> 00:36:01.730
[INAUDIBLE]

00:36:09.890 --> 00:36:13.050
In practice, system
identification

00:36:13.050 --> 00:36:15.050
leads to a number of questions.

00:36:15.050 --> 00:36:18.210
So we're describing this
overall framework or paradigm

00:36:18.210 --> 00:36:23.330
where we want to essentially
approximate the system

00:36:23.330 --> 00:36:26.610
dynamics by learning from data.

00:36:26.610 --> 00:36:28.410
And so some questions
that could emerge

00:36:28.410 --> 00:36:30.390
is, what if we
have a data budget?

00:36:30.390 --> 00:36:33.050
So how much data is
required to learn the model?

00:36:33.050 --> 00:36:36.810
Or how can we even quantify
what good means in terms

00:36:36.810 --> 00:36:39.450
of parameter estimation?

00:36:39.450 --> 00:36:44.570
And interestingly, so we
did all of these analyzes

00:36:44.570 --> 00:36:47.290
from the perspective of
a statistician wanting

00:36:47.290 --> 00:36:52.410
to analyze the performance or
the behavior of an estimator

00:36:52.410 --> 00:36:53.630
of our parameters.

00:36:53.630 --> 00:36:56.170
But, in reality, what we care
about is control performance.

00:36:56.170 --> 00:36:59.795
So we do not really care too
much about model accuracy.

00:36:59.795 --> 00:37:02.170
What we care about is that we
get a better controller out

00:37:02.170 --> 00:37:03.330
of this process.

00:37:03.330 --> 00:37:12.700
And so, again, just to make
a visual discussion here, so

00:37:12.700 --> 00:37:18.460
consider the case where we
have a very extreme case where

00:37:18.460 --> 00:37:24.100
we have our depending on some
parameter theta, which we don't

00:37:24.100 --> 00:37:26.820
really know the exact value
of, depending on the parameter

00:37:26.820 --> 00:37:29.720
theta, our control
law would vary wildly.

00:37:29.720 --> 00:37:32.200
So say that if
theta is negative,

00:37:32.200 --> 00:37:34.962
we would have one specific
control that we have to take.

00:37:34.962 --> 00:37:37.420
And if theta is positive, we
would have a different control

00:37:37.420 --> 00:37:38.295
that we have to take.

00:37:38.295 --> 00:37:39.980
So a very kind of
stepwise control

00:37:39.980 --> 00:37:42.460
function depending on theta.

00:37:42.460 --> 00:37:47.940
And say that the ground truth
value of theta is over here.

00:37:47.940 --> 00:37:53.900
Now, consider these
two different estimates

00:37:53.900 --> 00:37:57.940
for specifically theta
1 and theta hat 2

00:37:57.940 --> 00:38:01.060
for that same parameter theta.

00:38:01.060 --> 00:38:05.940
Of course, the one on the left
is from a purely statistical

00:38:05.940 --> 00:38:07.980
point of view or
at least distance

00:38:07.980 --> 00:38:11.100
based here is worse
because it's further off.

00:38:11.100 --> 00:38:13.380
But from the
perspective of control,

00:38:13.380 --> 00:38:17.070
since we are on the
right side of the sign,

00:38:17.070 --> 00:38:18.820
this would actually
lead to the controller

00:38:18.820 --> 00:38:21.500
having better performance.

00:38:21.500 --> 00:38:25.520
So, of course, this is a extreme
case and completely pictorial.

00:38:25.520 --> 00:38:28.320
But this is exactly the
intuition that I'm referring to.

00:38:32.020 --> 00:38:35.860
And, in a way, this is
one of the main downsides

00:38:35.860 --> 00:38:39.900
or bottlenecks of these
system identification

00:38:39.900 --> 00:38:41.620
approaches because
we are introducing

00:38:41.620 --> 00:38:44.380
this intermediate objective
that is model estimation, which

00:38:44.380 --> 00:38:47.280
is not necessarily aligned
with controller performance.

00:38:52.860 --> 00:38:56.180
Some other question
is, so we are assuming

00:38:56.180 --> 00:38:57.800
we have access to a data set.

00:38:57.800 --> 00:39:01.380
How do we collect this data set?

00:39:01.380 --> 00:39:05.170
What if we are learning
while we collect the data set

00:39:05.170 --> 00:39:07.910
or what if we have some
safety considerations that

00:39:07.910 --> 00:39:10.830
have to be done while
we collect the data set?

00:39:10.830 --> 00:39:13.010
Of course, we need to
take this into account

00:39:13.010 --> 00:39:15.310
because we would be
operating in a system

00:39:15.310 --> 00:39:17.810
with only partial knowledge
of what the system is.

00:39:17.810 --> 00:39:21.990
And so we might want to be
more and more conservative.

00:39:21.990 --> 00:39:25.470
And lastly, here we are
assuming we are doing everything

00:39:25.470 --> 00:39:27.690
under this linear assumption.

00:39:27.690 --> 00:39:29.490
So we have a ground
truth linear model,

00:39:29.490 --> 00:39:32.110
and we are trying to
estimate the parameters

00:39:32.110 --> 00:39:33.410
of a linear model.

00:39:33.410 --> 00:39:36.530
So the family of models
actually matches.

00:39:36.530 --> 00:39:38.510
But in reality, we
don't really know how

00:39:38.510 --> 00:39:40.130
the system behaves sometimes.

00:39:40.130 --> 00:39:42.790
And so there's an
entire discussion on,

00:39:42.790 --> 00:39:45.390
is our family of
models that we're

00:39:45.390 --> 00:39:49.630
trying to estimate expressive
enough to include the dynamics

00:39:49.630 --> 00:39:51.250
that we actually wish to learn?

00:39:51.250 --> 00:39:54.570
And so there is a trade-off
between how much data we have,

00:39:54.570 --> 00:39:57.330
what is the expressivity of
the model we want to learn,

00:39:57.330 --> 00:39:58.090
and so forth.

00:40:02.390 --> 00:40:05.990
Any questions on system
identification before we move on

00:40:05.990 --> 00:40:07.170
to adaptive control?

00:40:17.870 --> 00:40:20.550
I mean, I guess the main
idea here is pretty simple.

00:40:20.550 --> 00:40:22.790
So we want to estimate
a model from data

00:40:22.790 --> 00:40:28.510
and then use this potentially to
improve our controller later on.

00:40:28.510 --> 00:40:32.910
So let's move to
adaptive control.

00:40:32.910 --> 00:40:37.030
And if system identification
was essentially

00:40:37.030 --> 00:40:40.750
lying into this zero episodes
setting where we had this data

00:40:40.750 --> 00:40:42.710
and we wanted to use
this data to estimate

00:40:42.710 --> 00:40:46.270
the amount of our
dynamics, adaptive control

00:40:46.270 --> 00:40:50.070
falls into this
one episode setting

00:40:50.070 --> 00:40:54.550
whereby we want to improve
our control essentially

00:40:54.550 --> 00:40:58.030
through online
adaptation in real time

00:40:58.030 --> 00:41:00.230
as we operate the system.

00:41:00.230 --> 00:41:03.370
And so this can be done, again,
as we were saying before,

00:41:03.370 --> 00:41:06.230
by either directly
improving the controller

00:41:06.230 --> 00:41:09.190
or by first learning a model
and then using this model

00:41:09.190 --> 00:41:10.630
to improve the controller.

00:41:10.630 --> 00:41:13.590
And this is what differentiates
between model reference adaptive

00:41:13.590 --> 00:41:16.590
control, which essentially falls
in the first category, where

00:41:16.590 --> 00:41:19.390
as we will see, we are directly
improving the controller

00:41:19.390 --> 00:41:22.050
or model identification
adaptive control.

00:41:22.050 --> 00:41:24.990
So notice identification
also in the name

00:41:24.990 --> 00:41:27.070
which falls under
the second category

00:41:27.070 --> 00:41:28.790
where we are first
estimating a model

00:41:28.790 --> 00:41:33.470
and then using this to improve
the controller performance.

00:41:33.470 --> 00:41:37.670
And I would say that kind
of literature in adaptive

00:41:37.670 --> 00:41:41.250
control it's pretty traditional.

00:41:41.250 --> 00:41:44.190
So it dates back to a
number of years ago.

00:41:44.190 --> 00:41:47.350
And I would say that in
general, the classical setting

00:41:47.350 --> 00:41:50.870
is that it does not consider
the optimal adaptive control

00:41:50.870 --> 00:41:53.990
setting, whereby by optimal
I mean we are not necessarily

00:41:53.990 --> 00:41:57.590
optimizing for some
performance metric or cost,

00:41:57.590 --> 00:42:01.400
but I would say that adaptive
control strategies are typically

00:42:01.400 --> 00:42:06.820
focusing on proving the
stability of the coupled system,

00:42:06.820 --> 00:42:10.320
which entails the decoupling
of the controller,

00:42:10.320 --> 00:42:13.760
the adaptive component,
and the system dynamics.

00:42:13.760 --> 00:42:16.520
So how we will see is that we
won't be necessarily optimizing

00:42:16.520 --> 00:42:19.800
for some performance metric,
so minimization of some cost

00:42:19.800 --> 00:42:20.600
function.

00:42:20.600 --> 00:42:23.360
But we will be
studying the stability

00:42:23.360 --> 00:42:26.920
of the coupled closed
loop dynamics that

00:42:26.920 --> 00:42:30.480
emerge from the definition of
a specific controller, which

00:42:30.480 --> 00:42:33.260
we have to come up with
of an adaptive component.

00:42:33.260 --> 00:42:35.200
That is how we adapt
the controller based

00:42:35.200 --> 00:42:39.380
on experience, which we have to
decide, and the system dynamics.

00:42:45.280 --> 00:42:49.560
And so as a brief
recap and detour,

00:42:49.560 --> 00:42:53.480
the tool we are using throughout
this class to analyze stability

00:42:53.480 --> 00:42:57.200
is one of Lyapunov stability.

00:42:57.200 --> 00:43:00.640
And specifically, as
we discussed last week,

00:43:00.640 --> 00:43:03.720
we defined the Lyapunov
theorem as follows.

00:43:03.720 --> 00:43:08.480
So we're given an autonomous
system, x dot f of x.

00:43:08.480 --> 00:43:12.920
Note that we can easily
represent systems

00:43:12.920 --> 00:43:15.120
that are controlled
through some policy, x,

00:43:15.120 --> 00:43:19.200
as an autonomous system,
whereby we are directly encoding

00:43:19.200 --> 00:43:25.840
the effect of our control as a
function of the state, pi of x.

00:43:25.840 --> 00:43:27.800
So we're given an
autonomous system.

00:43:27.800 --> 00:43:30.800
And we have an
equilibrium point, x star,

00:43:30.800 --> 00:43:34.980
such that the dynamics f
at x star are equal to 0.

00:43:34.980 --> 00:43:38.240
So this is an equilibrium
point for our system.

00:43:38.240 --> 00:43:44.200
So last time we introduced
Lyapunov stability as this--

00:43:44.200 --> 00:43:46.760
we saw it from a
very pragmatic lens

00:43:46.760 --> 00:43:49.720
as this tool that enables
us to derive stability

00:43:49.720 --> 00:43:52.640
properties of the system.

00:43:52.640 --> 00:43:56.650
And we did this from the
perspective of evolve

00:43:56.650 --> 00:44:00.770
according to the Lyapunov
theorem for global stability,

00:44:00.770 --> 00:44:05.310
if there exists a function that
satisfies some characteristics,

00:44:05.310 --> 00:44:10.130
which we will see briefly, then
this equilibrium is globally

00:44:10.130 --> 00:44:12.010
asymptotically stable.

00:44:12.010 --> 00:44:13.650
I think last week
we only discussed

00:44:13.650 --> 00:44:15.470
the local versions of this.

00:44:15.470 --> 00:44:17.710
So we're not really looking
at the global stability,

00:44:17.710 --> 00:44:20.130
but we are reinforcing
this today.

00:44:20.130 --> 00:44:21.850
And specifically,
the conditions is

00:44:21.850 --> 00:44:26.010
that we need to find this
Lyapunov function, v,

00:44:26.010 --> 00:44:28.510
which respects these
three conditions.

00:44:28.510 --> 00:44:31.370
The first one is that v of x,
so it has to be the function v

00:44:31.370 --> 00:44:34.130
has to be positive
for all x that

00:44:34.130 --> 00:44:38.530
are not the equilibrium point
and 0 at the equilibrium point.

00:44:38.530 --> 00:44:40.890
The derivative, v
dot of x has to be

00:44:40.890 --> 00:44:43.850
negative for all points
that are not equilibrium.

00:44:43.850 --> 00:44:47.435
And the third property
and the third condition

00:44:47.435 --> 00:44:48.810
that we have to
respect, which is

00:44:48.810 --> 00:44:50.570
the one we are
using to strengthen

00:44:50.570 --> 00:44:53.170
our conditions for
global stability,

00:44:53.170 --> 00:44:56.170
and this is also known
as radial unboundedness,

00:44:56.170 --> 00:45:00.490
is that as the norm of
x grows to infinity,

00:45:00.490 --> 00:45:03.030
also the value of the
function grows to infinity.

00:45:05.770 --> 00:45:11.970
So visually, what
we're saying is that we

00:45:11.970 --> 00:45:15.630
have a system of n x1, x2.

00:45:18.970 --> 00:45:21.450
The Lyapunov
function is something

00:45:21.450 --> 00:45:26.970
that is like ball shaped
and that following

00:45:26.970 --> 00:45:31.330
the dynamics of the system,
it essentially dissipates

00:45:31.330 --> 00:45:35.150
energy and reaches
the equilibrium point.

00:45:35.150 --> 00:45:38.490
So this is positive everywhere
except at the equilibrium

00:45:38.490 --> 00:45:39.610
where it's 0.

00:45:39.610 --> 00:45:41.690
And the third
condition essentially

00:45:41.690 --> 00:45:45.470
says that this keeps growing
the more we go further out.

00:45:48.530 --> 00:45:50.730
And we discussed as
this was essentially

00:45:50.730 --> 00:45:54.220
an extremely useful way of
approaching the stability

00:45:54.220 --> 00:45:58.460
property, because we don't
have to integrate our dynamics

00:45:58.460 --> 00:46:00.590
or solve these for every
possible conditions,

00:46:00.590 --> 00:46:02.340
but we just have to
define a function that

00:46:02.340 --> 00:46:04.740
respects these
properties, and we

00:46:04.740 --> 00:46:08.080
can draw conclusions about the
stability of the system overall.

00:46:14.246 --> 00:46:15.640
Any questions here?

00:46:15.640 --> 00:46:17.440
This is mostly a repetition.

00:46:17.440 --> 00:46:20.560
But any questions on so far?

00:46:26.540 --> 00:46:30.020
What we'll be
doing going forward

00:46:30.020 --> 00:46:32.900
is that we'll be using
this Lyapunov stability

00:46:32.900 --> 00:46:38.340
theorem to prove stability
of coupled system dynamics

00:46:38.340 --> 00:46:41.400
controller and adaptive
component mechanisms.

00:46:41.400 --> 00:46:43.760
And so here we take
a little step back.

00:46:43.760 --> 00:46:45.220
We open this
parenthesis where we

00:46:45.220 --> 00:46:48.420
show exactly what we mean by
doing these Lyapunov stability

00:46:48.420 --> 00:46:52.300
analysis on a system that
doesn't really entail or control

00:46:52.300 --> 00:46:52.800
at all.

00:46:52.800 --> 00:46:56.620
But just to show you how we do
the stability analysis, which

00:46:56.620 --> 00:46:59.760
we will then be doing for the
purpose of adaptive control.

00:47:05.560 --> 00:47:10.320
So let me make some room here.

00:47:21.660 --> 00:47:27.700
And so let's consider the system
composed of a mass spring damper

00:47:27.700 --> 00:47:31.500
system where we have some mass,
m, on which we apply some force,

00:47:31.500 --> 00:47:32.500
f.

00:47:32.500 --> 00:47:37.200
We have a spring, k,
and we have a damper, d.

00:47:41.540 --> 00:47:50.060
We want to prove the stability
of this system to the zero

00:47:50.060 --> 00:47:55.030
point, specifically zero
position and zero velocity.

00:47:55.030 --> 00:47:57.270
And to do this, we
will assume and we

00:47:57.270 --> 00:47:59.070
will consider that the
energy of the system

00:47:59.070 --> 00:48:02.830
is our candidate
Lyapunov function.

00:48:02.830 --> 00:48:10.410
So the system is described by an
energy function, E of x, x dot,

00:48:10.410 --> 00:48:22.210
which is 1/2 k x dot plus 1/2 m
x dot squared, so essentially,

00:48:22.210 --> 00:48:26.350
a combination of potential
and kinetic energy.

00:48:26.350 --> 00:48:29.310
And so what we want to do is
given a candidate Lyapunov

00:48:29.310 --> 00:48:32.670
function for our system, we want
to check those three properties

00:48:32.670 --> 00:48:34.730
that we are describing
in the previous slide.

00:48:40.110 --> 00:48:43.510
So in this case, condition
1 and condition 3

00:48:43.510 --> 00:48:46.630
are easily derivable
by inspection.

00:48:46.630 --> 00:48:52.230
So we can see how this
energy function is positive

00:48:52.230 --> 00:48:57.750
for all x and x dot, unless
these are equal to 0,

00:48:57.750 --> 00:48:59.350
so our equilibrium point.

00:48:59.350 --> 00:49:04.110
And we can see that as x grows
also our energy function grows.

00:49:04.110 --> 00:49:05.870
And so we'll focus
on our analysis

00:49:05.870 --> 00:49:10.310
on proving the second of
the three conditions, that

00:49:10.310 --> 00:49:12.530
is the derivative
of our function,

00:49:12.530 --> 00:49:14.190
in this case, the
energy function,

00:49:14.190 --> 00:49:17.490
is negative for all points
that are not the equilibrium.

00:49:20.630 --> 00:49:22.770
So let us do this.

00:49:22.770 --> 00:49:26.710
So let's compute the derivative
of the energy function, E

00:49:26.710 --> 00:49:30.460
of x, x dot, which
is equal to kx,

00:49:30.460 --> 00:49:35.610
x dot plus m x dot x double dot.

00:49:44.310 --> 00:49:48.520
Here we can use the fact
that from our system dynamics

00:49:48.520 --> 00:49:54.840
that we know that m x dot
is equal to negative dx

00:49:54.840 --> 00:49:56.400
dot plus kx.

00:50:03.600 --> 00:50:11.400
Essentially rewrite this as
kx x dot minus x dot times

00:50:11.400 --> 00:50:26.000
dx dot plus kx, from which we
know that these two cancel out.

00:50:26.000 --> 00:50:28.560
And so we get,
essentially, the result

00:50:28.560 --> 00:50:31.580
that we have on the
slide, that is negative dx

00:50:31.580 --> 00:50:36.680
dot squared, which indeed
verifies our second property

00:50:36.680 --> 00:50:39.760
because this is indeed
always negative besides

00:50:39.760 --> 00:50:41.260
from the equilibrium point.

00:50:45.520 --> 00:50:50.160
So this is, indeed, what we
mean by performing a stability

00:50:50.160 --> 00:50:52.420
analysis through
Lyapunov theorems.

00:51:03.600 --> 00:51:07.880
So let us now move to what
we actually care about, which

00:51:07.880 --> 00:51:10.800
is adaptive control techniques.

00:51:10.800 --> 00:51:13.920
And so, specifically, let us
start from model reference

00:51:13.920 --> 00:51:16.920
adaptive control.

00:51:16.920 --> 00:51:19.000
So in model reference
adaptive control,

00:51:19.000 --> 00:51:23.200
we essentially will be defining
four key elements or parts.

00:51:23.200 --> 00:51:26.748
So the first one will be a plant
containing unknown parameters.

00:51:26.748 --> 00:51:28.540
So the plant here has
a very specific word.

00:51:28.540 --> 00:51:31.120
But let's consider the system.

00:51:31.120 --> 00:51:34.840
So this is the system where we
have some unknown parameters.

00:51:34.840 --> 00:51:39.240
We will be defining a reference
model, which essentially

00:51:39.240 --> 00:51:42.680
compactly specifies the
desired output that we would

00:51:42.680 --> 00:51:46.450
want to have from our system.

00:51:46.450 --> 00:51:48.570
In a way, the
reference model can

00:51:48.570 --> 00:51:51.650
be seen as an alternative
way to specify performance.

00:51:51.650 --> 00:51:53.570
So we're not really
measuring performance

00:51:53.570 --> 00:51:56.610
through the lens of a
metric or objective function

00:51:56.610 --> 00:51:58.190
that we wish to optimize.

00:51:58.190 --> 00:52:02.250
But measure performance
from the perspective

00:52:02.250 --> 00:52:05.230
of measuring distance with
respect to a reference signal.

00:52:08.050 --> 00:52:11.410
The third element is that we
will have a feedback control

00:52:11.410 --> 00:52:14.290
law that contains some
adjustable parameters.

00:52:14.290 --> 00:52:18.050
So we will be coming up with
a control mechanism or control

00:52:18.050 --> 00:52:21.950
policy, which depends on some
parameters which are adjustable.

00:52:24.770 --> 00:52:28.170
And, in particular, we will
be adjusting these parameters

00:52:28.170 --> 00:52:32.050
according to an adaptation
mechanisms which we'll

00:52:32.050 --> 00:52:34.510
be tasked with coming up with.

00:52:40.730 --> 00:52:42.430
Any question on the setting?

00:52:47.010 --> 00:52:50.090
OK, great.

00:52:50.090 --> 00:52:53.290
OK, so now we will be
going through an example

00:52:53.290 --> 00:52:55.310
of the analysis
that we're doing.

00:52:55.310 --> 00:52:59.450
We might be doing
in adaptive control.

00:52:59.450 --> 00:53:01.130
We will be, first
of all, setting up

00:53:01.130 --> 00:53:03.590
the stage in a setting
where everything is known.

00:53:03.590 --> 00:53:06.930
So we'll be just doing the
analysis when we actually

00:53:06.930 --> 00:53:11.210
don't have unknown parameters
and adaptation mechanisms.

00:53:11.210 --> 00:53:13.910
And then we will be
taking that same example

00:53:13.910 --> 00:53:17.810
but now adding this
adjusting component

00:53:17.810 --> 00:53:19.590
and repeating the analysis.

00:53:23.130 --> 00:53:30.450
But as a frame of
reference, remember

00:53:30.450 --> 00:53:33.010
that in adaptive
control, we care

00:53:33.010 --> 00:53:37.930
about measuring the stability
of the coupled system controller

00:53:37.930 --> 00:53:43.330
adaptation mechanism system,
so this coupled system.

00:53:43.330 --> 00:53:50.070
And so, in general, we will be
defining our four ingredients.

00:53:50.070 --> 00:53:52.330
So we'll have a
system which might

00:53:52.330 --> 00:53:57.270
have some unknown parameters.

00:54:00.170 --> 00:54:03.830
In this case, we consider
a double integrator system.

00:54:03.830 --> 00:54:09.110
So for us, the system will
be mx double dot equals u.

00:54:13.290 --> 00:54:17.810
We said before that we want to
measure performance as distance

00:54:17.810 --> 00:54:20.370
with respect to our
reference output

00:54:20.370 --> 00:54:22.730
that we wish to have
from our system.

00:54:22.730 --> 00:54:30.090
And specifically,
for us, the reference

00:54:30.090 --> 00:54:36.570
will be described through
fictitious variable, which

00:54:36.570 --> 00:54:42.460
we introduce, x sub m,
which represents essentially

00:54:42.460 --> 00:54:47.980
the version of our
state, x, which

00:54:47.980 --> 00:54:52.500
we create this kind
of fictitious model

00:54:52.500 --> 00:54:54.580
in order to represent.

00:54:54.580 --> 00:54:58.460
So we assume we have
someone giving us

00:54:58.460 --> 00:55:03.460
positioning command,
rt, and we come up

00:55:03.460 --> 00:55:08.780
with this ideal response of
the controlled mass, which

00:55:08.780 --> 00:55:11.920
we assume evolves according
to this differential equation.

00:55:11.920 --> 00:55:15.500
So we're introducing
this fictitious x sub m.

00:55:15.500 --> 00:55:20.300
And we hypothesize that our
system will evolve according

00:55:20.300 --> 00:55:21.480
to this reference model.

00:55:21.480 --> 00:55:23.840
So x sub m stands for model.

00:55:26.860 --> 00:55:28.980
So specifically,
our reference will

00:55:28.980 --> 00:55:36.380
be x double dot xm
double dot plus kd xm dot

00:55:36.380 --> 00:55:46.340
plus kp xm equals kr of t.

00:55:46.340 --> 00:55:51.140
So x sub m will be
our ideal output.

00:55:51.140 --> 00:55:53.020
The various ks
here are just gains

00:55:53.020 --> 00:55:55.420
we can choose as
a system designer.

00:55:55.420 --> 00:55:58.660
And we assume that r is
a position in command

00:55:58.660 --> 00:55:59.920
that is given to us.

00:56:05.460 --> 00:56:12.300
So now we assume that the
mass is known exactly.

00:56:12.300 --> 00:56:16.580
And we can basically
state the fact

00:56:16.580 --> 00:56:19.120
that if the mass
is known exactly,

00:56:19.120 --> 00:56:21.900
we know that we can
achieve a perfect tracking

00:56:21.900 --> 00:56:26.680
of our reference model
through that control law.

00:56:33.860 --> 00:56:39.510
So the controller specifically
is that u is equal to m times

00:56:39.510 --> 00:56:50.710
xm double dot minus 2 lambda x
tilde dot minus lambda squared

00:56:50.710 --> 00:56:59.150
x tilde, where we are
denoting as x tilde

00:56:59.150 --> 00:57:04.310
as the difference between the
output of the actual system

00:57:04.310 --> 00:57:06.193
and the reference,
the ideal output

00:57:06.193 --> 00:57:07.610
that we would want
from the system

00:57:07.610 --> 00:57:09.290
so if we want the
tracking error.

00:57:12.270 --> 00:57:14.390
So you see here
as we are building

00:57:14.390 --> 00:57:19.910
the different ingredients of
our adaptive control pipeline.

00:57:19.910 --> 00:57:25.410
And you might wonder like, where
is any of this coming from?

00:57:25.410 --> 00:57:28.030
The point is that
if we were actually

00:57:28.030 --> 00:57:30.950
having to design
the system, we would

00:57:30.950 --> 00:57:34.810
be tasked with designing
these things ourselves.

00:57:34.810 --> 00:57:37.538
So we would be
having to-- we would

00:57:37.538 --> 00:57:39.830
have to come up with a
reference signal, which we think

00:57:39.830 --> 00:57:41.550
is appropriate for our system.

00:57:41.550 --> 00:57:43.310
We would have to come
up with a control

00:57:43.310 --> 00:57:46.510
law which controls our
system, which again, we

00:57:46.510 --> 00:57:48.270
think is appropriate.

00:57:48.270 --> 00:57:50.110
But the purpose
of this is that we

00:57:50.110 --> 00:57:54.330
are assuming that someone gives
all of these elements to us,

00:57:54.330 --> 00:57:57.550
and we are showing the
kind of stability analysis

00:57:57.550 --> 00:58:00.390
that follows once we
have these ingredients.

00:58:00.390 --> 00:58:02.910
In reality, there is obviously
an iterative component

00:58:02.910 --> 00:58:05.090
where maybe we have a system.

00:58:05.090 --> 00:58:08.010
We try out a specific
type of reference model,

00:58:08.010 --> 00:58:11.150
we see how it works, and then
maybe this doesn't-- we're not

00:58:11.150 --> 00:58:14.450
really able to prove stability,
and we keep iterating on this.

00:58:17.630 --> 00:58:19.968
But for the purpose of
this stability analysis,

00:58:19.968 --> 00:58:21.510
this is the kind of
setting we're in.

00:58:21.510 --> 00:58:22.610
So we have a system.

00:58:22.610 --> 00:58:27.930
We define a reference
that we wish to track.

00:58:27.930 --> 00:58:33.270
And we have a control law
which exponentially converges

00:58:33.270 --> 00:58:36.240
to the zero tracking error.

00:58:36.240 --> 00:58:40.040
So to show that this is a
controller that basically

00:58:40.040 --> 00:58:42.040
converges to zero
tracking error,

00:58:42.040 --> 00:58:47.400
we have to derive the closed
loop dynamics of this combined

00:58:47.400 --> 00:58:48.100
system.

00:59:01.320 --> 00:59:03.020
Is it clear what we
are trying to do?

00:59:10.120 --> 00:59:12.820
So let us start from
our system dynamics.

00:59:12.820 --> 00:59:17.000
So mx double dot equals u.

00:59:17.000 --> 00:59:20.360
So we have just defined u
according to this controller.

00:59:20.360 --> 00:59:27.360
And so we can basically
replace this as mx double dot

00:59:27.360 --> 00:59:34.920
m minus 2 lambda x tilde
dot minus lambda squared

00:59:34.920 --> 00:59:47.520
x tilde which we can
rewrite as m times

00:59:47.520 --> 00:59:54.480
x double dot minus xm double
dot plus 2 lambda x tilde

00:59:54.480 --> 01:00:02.120
dot plus lambda squared x
tilde just to be equal to 0.

01:00:02.120 --> 01:00:05.380
So we just brought this
term on the other side.

01:00:10.720 --> 01:00:13.120
Now, from our definitions,
we know that this

01:00:13.120 --> 01:00:16.340
is x tilde double dot.

01:00:20.880 --> 01:00:24.120
And so we see that
we can essentially

01:00:24.120 --> 01:00:27.040
discard m because this
has to be equal to 0.

01:00:27.040 --> 01:00:32.570
And so if we solve this
ODE, the solution of the ODE

01:00:32.570 --> 01:00:43.330
is actually that x tilde t
is equal to c0 plus c1t times

01:00:43.330 --> 01:00:45.830
e to the negative lambda t.

01:00:53.950 --> 01:00:57.470
Now we know that lambda is
a gain, which we choose.

01:00:57.470 --> 01:00:59.810
And this gain is positive.

01:00:59.810 --> 01:01:04.170
So we know that lambda
here is positive.

01:01:04.170 --> 01:01:14.050
So what we can say is that as
t goes to infinity, then this--

01:01:14.050 --> 01:01:16.450
so x tilde t goes to 0.

01:01:26.490 --> 01:01:29.150
But what does this tell
us about tracking error?

01:01:33.410 --> 01:01:34.230
Yes.

01:01:34.230 --> 01:01:35.230
[INAUDIBLE]

01:01:35.230 --> 01:01:36.390
Yes, exactly.

01:01:36.390 --> 01:01:39.630
So we defined x tilde as
the tracking error itself.

01:01:39.630 --> 01:01:42.790
So we defined it as x minus xm.

01:01:42.790 --> 01:01:44.670
And we know that this goes to 0.

01:01:52.490 --> 01:01:57.210
So this was basically-- we
introduced the overall setting.

01:01:57.210 --> 01:02:00.350
We hypothesized the
mass, m, was known.

01:02:00.350 --> 01:02:02.530
And we basically said
that this control law

01:02:02.530 --> 01:02:06.850
was the controller that
guaranteed convergence to zero

01:02:06.850 --> 01:02:07.730
tracking error.

01:02:07.730 --> 01:02:11.170
And we have motivated it
by this analysis over here.

01:02:11.170 --> 01:02:13.950
Now we go into the setting
that we actually care about,

01:02:13.950 --> 01:02:16.650
which is the one where we
assume that the mass is not

01:02:16.650 --> 01:02:18.230
known exactly.

01:02:18.230 --> 01:02:22.930
So here we assume that
everything basically

01:02:22.930 --> 01:02:24.410
stays the same.

01:02:24.410 --> 01:02:27.490
But now we are in the slightly
more realistic setting

01:02:27.490 --> 01:02:29.980
where the mass is not known.

01:02:29.980 --> 01:02:33.120
And so we will have an
estimate for the mass, m hat.

01:02:43.240 --> 01:02:48.980
So now, let us once again
derive the closed loop dynamics

01:02:48.980 --> 01:02:50.640
of this combined system.

01:03:04.740 --> 01:03:09.140
And to do this, let us
do some definitions just

01:03:09.140 --> 01:03:11.080
for notational compactness.

01:03:11.080 --> 01:03:18.900
So we will define s as x
tilde dot plus lambda x tilde.

01:03:18.900 --> 01:03:26.580
We'll define nu as x double
dot minus 2 lambda x tilde dot

01:03:26.580 --> 01:03:30.260
minus lambda squared x tilde.

01:03:30.260 --> 01:03:35.840
And we will define m tilde
as the estimation error,

01:03:35.840 --> 01:03:38.440
basically, m hat minus m.

01:03:41.380 --> 01:03:45.580
So given these definitions,
let us essentially

01:03:45.580 --> 01:03:48.260
do the exact same
analysis we did over here.

01:03:48.260 --> 01:03:52.540
So we will derive the closed
loop dynamics of this combined

01:03:52.540 --> 01:03:55.260
system.

01:03:55.260 --> 01:04:01.780
So we start, once again, from
our dynamics mx dot u equals u.

01:04:01.780 --> 01:04:05.740
We know what u is because
this is something we define.

01:04:05.740 --> 01:04:09.140
But this time u is a function
of the unknown mass, m hat.

01:04:13.180 --> 01:04:24.900
So m hat times xm double dot
minus 2 lambda x tilde dot

01:04:24.900 --> 01:04:28.090
minus lambda squared x tilde.

01:04:32.590 --> 01:04:33.410
This is nu.

01:04:38.714 --> 01:04:53.710
And so we get m tilde nu
plus m nu, where basically,

01:04:53.710 --> 01:04:58.430
here we're using the
fact that m hat is

01:04:58.430 --> 01:05:06.450
equal to m tilde plus m from
the way we defined m tilde.

01:05:10.390 --> 01:05:13.790
And so this leads to our
closed loop dynamics defined

01:05:13.790 --> 01:05:22.510
as mx times x double
dot minus nu m tilde

01:05:22.510 --> 01:05:37.110
nu, which we can
expand as m times

01:05:37.110 --> 01:05:44.470
x double dot minus xm double
dot plus 2 lambda x tilde

01:05:44.470 --> 01:05:51.410
dot plus lambda squared
x tilde equals m tilde

01:05:51.410 --> 01:06:03.890
nu, where this is x double dot.

01:06:09.710 --> 01:06:13.390
And so we basically get
what we have on the slide.

01:06:13.390 --> 01:06:22.570
So we have m times s dot
plus lambda s equals m tilde.

01:06:29.720 --> 01:06:33.540
So nothing too deep here, just
some algebraic manipulations.

01:06:33.540 --> 01:06:37.660
But here we are, again,
defining this coupled system.

01:06:37.660 --> 01:06:40.000
And we are computing
the closed loop dynamics

01:06:40.000 --> 01:06:41.220
of this coupled system.

01:06:48.040 --> 01:06:53.200
So now what we want
to do is considering

01:06:53.200 --> 01:06:57.680
a specific way of adjusting
our estimate, m hat.

01:06:57.680 --> 01:06:59.760
So remember that we
are interested in doing

01:06:59.760 --> 01:07:00.980
these adaptations.

01:07:00.980 --> 01:07:04.900
And so we need an adaptive
component in the picture.

01:07:08.800 --> 01:07:11.200
And specifically,
we assume that we

01:07:11.200 --> 01:07:17.880
will be adjusting our
estimate, m hat, as according

01:07:17.880 --> 01:07:23.040
to this law that is m hat dot
is equal to negative gamma nu

01:07:23.040 --> 01:07:28.540
s, where gamma is, again, which
we call an adaptation gain.

01:07:28.540 --> 01:07:29.740
It has to be positive.

01:07:38.340 --> 01:07:41.040
So basically, now we have
all of the ingredients

01:07:41.040 --> 01:07:44.840
to perform our stability
analysis through Lyapunov.

01:07:44.840 --> 01:07:46.480
So what we're going
to do is we will

01:07:46.480 --> 01:07:49.600
be considering this
candidate Lyapunov function.

01:07:49.600 --> 01:07:53.560
And we will use this
to basically verify

01:07:53.560 --> 01:07:56.400
that the system is
globally asymptotically

01:07:56.400 --> 01:07:58.620
stable to an equilibrium point.

01:08:01.178 --> 01:08:02.720
And we'll study what
this equilibrium

01:08:02.720 --> 01:08:04.340
point means, essentially.

01:08:14.560 --> 01:08:20.279
So we have this candidate
Lyapunov function defined as 1/2

01:08:20.279 --> 01:08:30.080
times ms squared plus 1
over gamma m tilde squared.

01:08:30.080 --> 01:08:32.939
Remember that according
to our Lyapunov analysis,

01:08:32.939 --> 01:08:42.520
we have to check for our three
properties, which are, again,

01:08:42.520 --> 01:08:44.120
these ones here on the slide.

01:08:44.120 --> 01:08:47.859
So once again, the first one and
the third one are easy to prove.

01:08:47.859 --> 01:08:50.479
And I'll leave this
up to you guys.

01:08:50.479 --> 01:08:52.960
And so we'll focus on
the second one, that

01:08:52.960 --> 01:08:58.840
is that the derivative of
this Lyapunov function, v dot,

01:08:58.840 --> 01:09:01.160
has to be negative
for all points that

01:09:01.160 --> 01:09:02.939
are not the equilibrium.

01:09:05.920 --> 01:09:10.500
And so we'll be computing
the derivative, v dot,

01:09:10.500 --> 01:09:16.760
which is equal to ms s
dot plus 1 over gamma m

01:09:16.760 --> 01:09:19.180
tilde m tilde dot.

01:09:27.729 --> 01:09:32.290
So here we use the fact that
m tilde, as we defined it,

01:09:32.290 --> 01:09:35.189
is equal to m hat minus m.

01:09:38.890 --> 01:09:45.050
But because m is a fixed
mass, so it's a constant.

01:09:45.050 --> 01:09:49.870
From this, we can say that m
tilde dot is equal m hat dot.

01:10:01.570 --> 01:10:12.610
And so what we get is ms times
s dot plus 1 over gamma m tilde

01:10:12.610 --> 01:10:21.290
times m hat dot, which is
equal to ms s dot minus 1

01:10:21.290 --> 01:10:31.850
over gamma times m
tilde gamma nu s,

01:10:31.850 --> 01:10:37.170
where here we just used
basically the fact that m hat

01:10:37.170 --> 01:10:42.610
dot is equal to
negative gamma nu

01:10:42.610 --> 01:10:46.510
s according to how we define
the adaptation component.

01:10:56.930 --> 01:10:59.250
Which essentially
leads to the fact

01:10:59.250 --> 01:11:02.290
that the derivative of our
Lyapunov function, v dot,

01:11:02.290 --> 01:11:27.740
is equal to s times ms dot
minus m tilde nu, where

01:11:27.740 --> 01:11:33.020
this is minus m lambda s.

01:11:33.020 --> 01:11:37.220
And so we basically get
the result on the slide.

01:11:37.220 --> 01:11:41.200
So we know that
we can write here.

01:11:45.460 --> 01:11:53.700
Yes, so minus
lambda m s squared,

01:11:53.700 --> 01:11:59.980
which basically confirms
the second-- validates

01:11:59.980 --> 01:12:04.580
the second property specifically
as a function of s in this case.

01:12:04.580 --> 01:12:07.100
And so what this is
saying is that since we

01:12:07.100 --> 01:12:11.660
have been done this Lyapunov
stability given this candidate

01:12:11.660 --> 01:12:14.380
Lyapunov function,
what we can say

01:12:14.380 --> 01:12:34.060
is that we can say that s goes
to 0 because we are considering

01:12:34.060 --> 01:12:38.300
this as our candidate
equilibrium point.

01:12:38.300 --> 01:12:39.640
So s goes to 0.

01:12:39.640 --> 01:12:42.820
And so remember
how we defined s,

01:12:42.820 --> 01:12:48.160
which is x tilde dot
plus lambda x tilde.

01:12:51.780 --> 01:12:54.060
What we're basically saying
is that we have shown

01:12:54.060 --> 01:13:12.020
that this quantity goes to 0,
which means that x tilde dot is

01:13:12.020 --> 01:13:15.150
equal to negative
x tilde, lambda x

01:13:15.150 --> 01:13:22.530
tilde where lambda is positive.

01:13:22.530 --> 01:13:24.590
So remember lambda is positive.

01:13:24.590 --> 01:13:27.850
And so we've shown
that s goes to 0.

01:13:27.850 --> 01:13:32.830
And so this means that x
tilde dot tends to this.

01:13:32.830 --> 01:13:34.270
And so visually,
what we're seeing

01:13:34.270 --> 01:13:39.230
is that our system if we
were to express it in a plot

01:13:39.230 --> 01:13:43.110
as a function of x
tilde and x tilde dot

01:13:43.110 --> 01:13:49.270
is something that converges
to the line x tilde dot

01:13:49.270 --> 01:13:54.170
equals negative lambda x tilde.

01:13:54.170 --> 01:13:58.150
So it's something that as t
goes to infinity converges

01:13:58.150 --> 01:13:59.010
to this line.

01:14:02.550 --> 01:14:07.050
And specifically,
once it's on the line,

01:14:07.050 --> 01:14:12.270
if x tilde is negative,
the derivative of x tilde

01:14:12.270 --> 01:14:14.090
will be positive.

01:14:16.790 --> 01:14:18.670
And so it will tend to grow.

01:14:18.670 --> 01:14:21.910
On the other hand, if
x tilde is positive,

01:14:21.910 --> 01:14:24.090
the derivative will be negative.

01:14:24.090 --> 01:14:26.750
So it will tend to decrease.

01:14:26.750 --> 01:14:29.030
And so we have shown that
the system will essentially

01:14:29.030 --> 01:14:30.530
be converging to this line.

01:14:30.530 --> 01:14:33.970
And once on this line,
it will be going to 0.

01:14:41.630 --> 01:14:42.830
Why do we care about this?

01:14:42.830 --> 01:14:46.950
Is because if x tilde goes
to 0, we know that x tilde

01:14:46.950 --> 01:14:48.610
is our tracking
error, essentially.

01:14:48.610 --> 01:14:51.710
And so x minus xm
also goes to 0.

01:14:58.710 --> 01:15:00.145
Yes.

01:15:00.145 --> 01:15:03.450
[INAUDIBLE]

01:15:07.590 --> 01:15:10.960
So here we were using the
tool of Lyapunov stability

01:15:10.960 --> 01:15:14.560
to essentially prove stability
of the system to an equilibrium

01:15:14.560 --> 01:15:15.720
point.

01:15:15.720 --> 01:15:20.780
Specifically, this Lyapunov
candidate, Lyapunov function

01:15:20.780 --> 01:15:27.360
is defined as such, which
is basically a function of s

01:15:27.360 --> 01:15:28.800
in this case here.

01:15:28.800 --> 01:15:32.280
And so we have
verified explicitly

01:15:32.280 --> 01:15:35.040
the second of the three
properties but the other three

01:15:35.040 --> 01:15:38.920
are more easily shown by
even just by inspection.

01:15:38.920 --> 01:15:43.240
And we know that if a candidate
Lyapunov function satisfies

01:15:43.240 --> 01:15:46.680
these three properties, it shows
that the system will be globally

01:15:46.680 --> 01:15:50.800
asymptotically stable to
the equilibrium point, which

01:15:50.800 --> 01:15:55.480
in this case for us was
showing that the system would

01:15:55.480 --> 01:15:57.920
essentially be
globally asymptotically

01:15:57.920 --> 01:16:01.400
converging to s going to 0.

01:16:01.400 --> 01:16:03.400
And we showed,
then, this analysis

01:16:03.400 --> 01:16:07.320
whereby if s is going to 0, then
the tracking error will also

01:16:07.320 --> 01:16:08.420
be going to 0.

01:16:08.420 --> 01:16:10.600
So this is, in a way,
like a one to one

01:16:10.600 --> 01:16:15.185
mapping and application of the
Lyapunov stability theorem.

01:16:15.185 --> 01:16:16.060
Does that make sense?

01:16:19.800 --> 01:16:21.400
Yes.

01:16:21.400 --> 01:16:24.680
In this case with unknown
mass in the previous case

01:16:24.680 --> 01:16:30.160
with known mass is proof
convergence stability but not

01:16:30.160 --> 01:16:33.940
actually computing gamma
or computing lambda?

01:16:33.940 --> 01:16:34.440
No.

01:16:34.440 --> 01:16:38.960
So indeed, let me
take a step back.

01:16:38.960 --> 01:16:43.360
So specifically, the general
setting we're working with

01:16:43.360 --> 01:16:46.120
is we are introducing
adaptive control.

01:16:46.120 --> 01:16:48.760
And we mentioned that
classically, adaptive control

01:16:48.760 --> 01:16:52.200
is instantiated as
having to come up

01:16:52.200 --> 01:16:57.400
with a coupled system of
different ingredients, namely

01:16:57.400 --> 01:17:01.160
a system, the controller,
and an adaptation mechanism

01:17:01.160 --> 01:17:03.400
of some adjustable parameters.

01:17:03.400 --> 01:17:06.160
And the entire problem
will be posited

01:17:06.160 --> 01:17:09.570
as we want to derive
and prove the stability

01:17:09.570 --> 01:17:11.050
of this coupled system.

01:17:11.050 --> 01:17:15.730
So specifically, what
we have shown before

01:17:15.730 --> 01:17:20.370
is that we basically
constructed towards this example

01:17:20.370 --> 01:17:23.370
by first introducing the
case where m was known,

01:17:23.370 --> 01:17:27.890
and we just proved the stability
of that overall system.

01:17:27.890 --> 01:17:32.610
Now, we are introducing an
adaptation mechanism in order

01:17:32.610 --> 01:17:34.810
to adjust our estimate of m.

01:17:34.810 --> 01:17:38.530
And these adaptation
mechanisms rely on some gains

01:17:38.530 --> 01:17:40.550
that we are introducing.

01:17:40.550 --> 01:17:44.490
In practice, we would
have to tune this gain

01:17:44.490 --> 01:17:47.330
to answer your
question more directly.

01:17:47.330 --> 01:17:49.010
So I'm aware that this--

01:17:49.010 --> 01:17:52.410
I would say this
analysis can feel

01:17:52.410 --> 01:17:54.370
somewhat intricate and
complex in the sense

01:17:54.370 --> 01:17:58.610
that we are introducing a lot
of functions, which is kind

01:17:58.610 --> 01:18:00.070
of unclear where it comes from.

01:18:00.070 --> 01:18:03.330
We're introducing control
law, which is also unclear

01:18:03.330 --> 01:18:06.610
where it comes from, and
same thing for the reference.

01:18:06.610 --> 01:18:11.110
The reality is that if we
were to study this explicitly,

01:18:11.110 --> 01:18:14.675
this would be an extremely
case-specific discussion.

01:18:14.675 --> 01:18:16.550
And so I think for the
purpose of this class,

01:18:16.550 --> 01:18:17.770
not very meaningful.

01:18:17.770 --> 01:18:20.250
So the reality is that
whenever we have a system

01:18:20.250 --> 01:18:23.370
and we wish to apply adaptive
control for the system,

01:18:23.370 --> 01:18:28.410
we will have to be doing some
studies and analysis on ways

01:18:28.410 --> 01:18:31.490
of deriving all of
these components,

01:18:31.490 --> 01:18:33.710
deriving the reference,
deriving the control law,

01:18:33.710 --> 01:18:36.730
deciding how I want to adapt
my adjustable parameters,

01:18:36.730 --> 01:18:41.730
and also deriving a candidate
Lyapunov function, so a lot

01:18:41.730 --> 01:18:43.010
to take in.

01:18:43.010 --> 01:18:46.210
But then what we
decided to focus on

01:18:46.210 --> 01:18:49.530
for the purpose of this
class is showing that once

01:18:49.530 --> 01:18:51.650
we have all of these
ingredients, what

01:18:51.650 --> 01:18:57.210
are the stability analysis
that come as a result?

01:18:57.210 --> 01:18:59.330
Yes.

01:18:59.330 --> 01:19:01.570
This analysis would
[INAUDIBLE] hinge

01:19:01.570 --> 01:19:04.680
on that particular
Lyapunov function, right?

01:19:04.680 --> 01:19:05.180
Yes.

01:19:05.180 --> 01:19:06.380
How did you get that?

01:19:06.380 --> 01:19:06.880
Yeah.

01:19:06.880 --> 01:19:10.220
This is the point where we
are completely fast tracking.

01:19:10.220 --> 01:19:13.540
The reality is that if you
have an interesting system

01:19:13.540 --> 01:19:15.180
and you derive a
Lyapunov function

01:19:15.180 --> 01:19:16.840
and you show that
the system is stable,

01:19:16.840 --> 01:19:19.620
you write a paper about it,
and you're happy about it.

01:19:19.620 --> 01:19:24.700
So this is something where
it's extremely case dependent.

01:19:24.700 --> 01:19:26.160
We won't go into it.

01:19:26.160 --> 01:19:28.900
I think there are a number of
references that I'm actually

01:19:28.900 --> 01:19:31.320
I think the next slide
is exactly on this.

01:19:31.320 --> 01:19:33.100
So I definitely
advise you to look

01:19:33.100 --> 01:19:37.800
into this reference over here
to basically see if you wish,

01:19:37.800 --> 01:19:40.603
the forward direction
of this discussion.

01:19:40.603 --> 01:19:42.020
So until now, we've
basically been

01:19:42.020 --> 01:19:45.260
assuming that either us,
a previous version of us

01:19:45.260 --> 01:19:48.160
or someone incredibly smart, is
giving us all of these things,

01:19:48.160 --> 01:19:49.320
and we just analyze it.

01:19:49.320 --> 01:19:52.140
But in practice, we
have to come up with it.

01:19:52.140 --> 01:19:54.280
All right, so I'm out of time.

01:19:54.280 --> 01:19:57.640
So conclude the discussion
of MRAC next time,

01:19:57.640 --> 01:20:00.110
and see you on Wednesday.

